# tools/cr/crypto_kumara_attestation_witness.rish — the Kumara attestation, Season G's TWENTIETH composition: the parent→subkey attestation, a
# signed delegation. ed25519/SLIP-0010 admits only HARDENED derivation, so a subkey's public key cannot be derived from the parent identity's
# public key alone; this rung closes that boundary the one honest way — the identity SIGNS "the subkey named here, at this record, is mine," and a
# verifier checks it against the identity's public key (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md; crypto/kumara_subkey.rye names this rung outright
# as "a signed delegation over exactly the door crypto/kumara_carry.rye already opens." The 1,024-round itinerary, Season 2 Journey 6 (Provenance):
# an attested subkey is how one identity's chain of custody reaches, through many per-record keys, to a record a stranger can trust to the identity.
# Kin: crypto/kumara_subkey.rye (the subkey this attests, GREEN) · crypto/kumara_identity.rye (the identity that issues the attestation, GREEN) ·
# crypto/ed25519_sign.rye + crypto/ed25519_verify.rye (the signature over the statement, GREEN) · crypto/kumara_carry.rye (the record-open door
# this chains to, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET of the primitives beneath, never a copied line — our
# own composition, studied clean-room (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/kumara_attestation.rye authors NO new cryptography — the signature is ed25519_sign/verify; this module adds the CERTIFICATE (magic 'GKA1'
# ‖ issuer ‖ record u64 BE ‖ subkey ‖ Ed25519 signature-over-statement) and the binding that refuses any certificate whose issuer is not the
# identity the reader named, then chains to kumara_carry's open_as. Purely local over TEST identities from published vectors — no real identity key,
# no network, no funds, no real device; issuing a REAL attestation with the maintainer's own identity stays the custody gate, and constant-time
# timing-safety stays the named horizon.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_kumara_attestation_witness.rish

say "crypto-kumara-attestation: Language EN — Season G's TWENTIETH composition: the parent→subkey attestation, a signed delegation. crypto/kumara_subkey.rye left one honest boundary — ed25519/SLIP-0010 admits only HARDENED derivation, so a subkey's public key cannot be derived from the parent identity's public key alone, and a verifier holding only the identity's public key cannot on their own prove a subkey descends from it. This rung closes that the one honest way: the identity SIGNS 'the subkey named here, at this record, is mine,' and a verifier checks that signature against the identity's public key."
say "crypto-kumara-attestation: Style Radiant — one identity, many per-record subkeys, each provably its own to anyone holding only the identity's public key. The certificate is the exact shape the Kumara carry already established for a sealed record (magic → named-key binding → verify-before-trust), lifted from 'who sealed this record' to 'who vouches this subkey is theirs,' so the two compose into one chain: identity → attested subkey → sealed record."
say "crypto-kumara-attestation: Lens TAME — the composition authors no new cryptography; it wires a proven ed25519 signature to a proven carry door, fixes the certificate at 140 bytes, and asserts its contracts (the issuer re-derives and confirms the subkey before signing; the statement layout is written one way and read the same way; the returned issuer is the expected one). GREEN on metal with no fabricated constant: anchored (an identity from BIP-39's Trezor vector attests its own subkey; verify_attestation returns exactly that subkey's public key at record 0, and an independent ed25519_verify over the reconstructed statement agrees); the whole point (the certificate delivers a subkey public key a public-only holder could not have derived); the binding (refuses the wrong issuer, a flipped statement byte, a flipped signature byte, bad magic, a bad length, an undersized out buffer, each by its own named error); the chain (a record sealed by the attested subkey opens, a SIBLING subkey's record is refused UnexpectedSigner, a DIFFERENT identity's expectation is refused UnexpectedIssuer before the frame is opened); a forged delegation refused UnexpectedIssuer; determinism and per-record distinctness. Purely local — TEST identities only, no real identity key, no network, no funds, no device; issuing a real attestation stays the custody gate."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/kumara_attestation"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/kumara_attestation.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-kumara-attestation: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/kumara_attestation.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-kumara-attestation: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-kumara-attestation: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-kumara-attestation' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-kumara-attestation: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-kumara-attestation line"
